orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t\\cdot (6t-3)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{[4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}}{{[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]}_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-3)-0}{(12-18+9)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖2t-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨1,1⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{1}=1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133434 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362028 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134571 1071 "Let $V = C[0, 1]$, the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0, 1]$. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$,\nfind the distance between $f(t) = 6t$ and $g(t) = 5t^{2}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134571 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=6t$ and $g(t)=5{t}^{2}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134571 1074 "The distance between $f(t)=6t$ and $g(t)=5{t}^{2}$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-5{t}^{2}‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}-6t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5{t}^{2}-6t,5{t}^{2}-6t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5{t}^{2}-6t,5{t}^{2}-6t⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-6t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(25{t}^{4}-60{t}^{3}+36{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}-15{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(5-15+12)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133440 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362211 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134572 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$r(t) = ⟨t + \\sin (\\frac{πt}{2}), t + \\cos (\\frac{πt}{2})⟩, 0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$ for $F = xy^{2}i + x^{2}yj$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134572 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134572 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133446 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362118 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134573 1071 "Let $f(x, y, z) = x\\sin y + y\\cos z$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t) = ⟨\\sin t, t, 2t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186134573 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186134573 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362252 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362253 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362304 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362305 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362308 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362309 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362310 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362311 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362312 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362271 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362272 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362319 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362320 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362321 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362322 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362324 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362325 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362328 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362329 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362330 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362331 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362332 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362333 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362334 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362335 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362336 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362337 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362338 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147104 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362149 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148230 1071 "![](images/ab/ab2ec609e7add6dc3a44fc2f70fad4d4da325daf5614d80941ad499254962e14.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148230 1071 "![](images/ab/ab2ec609e7add6dc3a44fc2f70fad4d4da325daf5614d80941ad499254962e14.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$, where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148230 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-1,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-1,3).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/80/8073b3575bd48710fe7744221dbebe7f9d0656991ea4a9bea8e11e06431f63d7.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147128 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362182 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148234 1071 "![](images/97/97d1a6a7f167b29a12e2f586067f6d7fa3cb5afec0be0ae854512cfe5c968d42.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a source.\n- $y=-4$ is a sink." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148234 1071 "![](images/97/97d1a6a7f167b29a12e2f586067f6d7fa3cb5afec0be0ae854512cfe5c968d42.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y = 2$ is a node.\n2. $y = 2$ is a source.\n3. $y =-4$ is a sink." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148234 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7ad82b4ff48bbf69efccf5a822c7013929224a1a6e6e63e1948ea9ea95dcc858.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-4$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147134 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362015 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148235 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y^{′} = (y + 4)(y^{2} - 4)$?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148235 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+4)({y}^{2}-4)?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148235 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)({y}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y+2)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-2,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=\\pm 2.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-4,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-2,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/87/8756839e7b9c77934acec128d098eab5b7482a3b7422945b3507b6379fbc528b.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147140 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362099 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148236 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y =-2$ is a source\n- $y = 0$ is a sink\n- $y = 2$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186148236 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n\n- $y=2$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186148236 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is also a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](images/b5/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/fb/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186162050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362264 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186162050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362265 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186162050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362266 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362358 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362371 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362374 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362387 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362339 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362215 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186166895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362216 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186167550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166478 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186167550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362059 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186167550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186167550 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186167550 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}}⟨t, 2, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186167550 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362277 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362278 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362279 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362226 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172046 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362284 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172046 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362285 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172047 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362217 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362227 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362228 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362229 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362230 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362231 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362232 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362233 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362234 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362235 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362236 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362237 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362238 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362239 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362240 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362241 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362287 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362288 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362289 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362290 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362291 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362295 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362296 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362302 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362315 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362316 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171525 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361995 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172655 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2}(y-2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172655 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x + 1)^{2}(y - 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y > 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172655 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f4b1b2ca736fb83eff0a4b85a3f479bf99b3b337492d0a77abe739038ed141f.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/fd/fdafadd1631a75c2998aaed5c6bad62a3ea1dcc0ac57d245a00d167e74aa3bb4.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171537 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362098 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172657 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t) = 2\\cos 3ti + 2\\sin 3tj$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172657 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172657 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡3t+36{cos}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡3t+{cos}^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171543 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362193 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172658 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t) = 3(\\cos 2ti + \\sin 2tj)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172658 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172658 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡2t+144{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={2}^{2}\\cdot 3=12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171567 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362090 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172662 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172662 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - y$?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172662 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1),$ and $(1,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=(1)^{2}-(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=(1)^{2}-(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-2)}=(-2)^{2}-(-2)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f99159626fad30330210592c4e36859bfc30d7d3cf2c3f9178e1cb93498aa.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171585 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362196 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172665 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](images/2a/2a0662ed8b6892d3e1269ac07c8ea1f5700431c0643d2e71a5436719bf535f76.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171609 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362084 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172669 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise. \n\nAmong the given options, the only positively oriented simple closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/25/25c64f477f32bcef51f9198b076eed240e6be8b42991bbf3c8407209d6efc908.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171615 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362100 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172670 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171621 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362184 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172671 1074 "Let $z$ be a $12$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{12}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{12}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/12}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171633 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362061 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172673 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/72/72f59260e2d8e20a163fb4462a6332c4fafd00078a6e851806b30b9c005a0ae3.png)\n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/72/72aac3f5d5f3b58e571df5c2b8d35523af379dabf66b40c3018eba573b119fca.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171645 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186172675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362057 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186172675 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains two holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186198372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362354 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186198373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362348 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186198411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362352 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186198411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362360 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186199024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197984 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186199024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362072 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186199024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186199024 1071 "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48cm^{2}$. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." 13194139533434 17592186045585 false] [17592186199024 1071 "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186199024 1074 "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362347 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360835 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360835 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360835 1084 #uuid "0034a4c6-10b3-508b-8a76-afcee44b5628" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360836 1081 "36\\,j" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360836 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360836 1084 #uuid "00559944-b824-562d-ab1f-1d6d34ee529e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360837 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360837 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186360837 1084 #uuid "00a864a5-90fe-5a19-9ac4-88e0769c548c" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [1