\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362112 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1069 "q-97593" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1072 #uuid "24945e7c-95e1-50bc-b79f-c2e7d2556ce8" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362224 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362157 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1069 "q-97594" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1072 #uuid "4602817c-a855-5ccd-8d1a-bca1a25a4651" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362225 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362083 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1069 "q-97620" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3y+2?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1072 #uuid "e7c5af7c-5fc9-5568-9c77-70265ceb627b" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362226 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,2),(3,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,2)}=-3(2)+2=-4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=-3(-1)+2=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/e9/e98bd7245cbbd5854bd461a8fa1bbb42cef768ee842e71cc3372fa7fed85d804.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362105 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1069 "q-97631" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1071 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $1\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1072 #uuid "4a58d6f2-a102-5329-9df6-5ca2570034eb" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362227 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(4,0),$ we have that $r(0)=4i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=4.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362163 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1069 "q-97632" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1071 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(8,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1072 #uuid "0696e9be-8b02-58ba-8734-5aa777937265" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362228 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(8,0),$ we have that $r(0)=8i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=8.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=8$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362062 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1069 "q-97633" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1071 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1072 #uuid "4fda32b4-246d-55a1-b23a-5546b7947af1" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362229 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(3,0),$ we have that $r(0)=3i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=3.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362016 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1069 "q-97635" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1072 #uuid "b16e9211-db14-525d-9836-dc3a678dd49a" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362230 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(2,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,2i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=2.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=2$ and $\\omega =-3\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362113 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1069 "q-97638" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\pi \\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(6,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1072 #uuid "413896c1-f8f6-5231-affe-069c85d5ca17" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362231 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(6,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,6i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=6.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=6$ and $\\omega =-\\pi \\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi t)\\,j) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362154 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1069 "q-97639" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1072 #uuid "72654fcd-2f4f-5204-9c2a-8d41c0e3c763" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362232 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=4\\cdot |1|=4\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362089 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1069 "q-97640" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1072 #uuid "67551cd4-7a9e-5be3-84a9-bfa16e8db64b" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362233 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{sin}^{2}⁡2t+100{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $10\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=5\\cdot |2|=10\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362054 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1069 "q-97642" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1072 #uuid "dd3a57cc-829f-5f80-bbe3-87669029da3c" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362234 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t)^{2}+(6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+36{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t)} \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\pi \\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=3\\cdot |2\\pi |=6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362111 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1069 "q-97663" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $8\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1072 #uuid "5ce19249-a09b-54e8-9301-9d45138b727e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362235 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =8\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,i+24\