139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362012 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1069 "q-97710" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1071 "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1072 #uuid "e749e029-fcca-5f1a-8ba3-acd96fb75dcc" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362242 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362073 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1069 "q-97712" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1071 "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1072 #uuid "415714b2-f118-53c1-b9e3-24f1aa607a09" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362243 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }((2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(6\\pi +0+0)-(3\\pi +0+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362207 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1069 "q-97715" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1071 "Let $V=C[0,4]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,4],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{4}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}+1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1072 #uuid "d72a0577-32b3-5426-85c1-90f3346303ef" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362244 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\sqrt{t}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t+2\\sqrt{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4t\\sqrt{t}}{3}+t)|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{32}{3}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362022 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1069 "q-97721" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=2-3t.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1072 #uuid "4bb15900-4898-54df-9190-c765e91944f3" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362245 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2-3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(4-12t+9{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(4t-6{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4-6+3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3t}{1} \\\\  & =2-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362047 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1069 "q-97724" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1072 #uuid "074428bb-c6cc-5e14-8779-13ee23218f8c" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362246 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1+12+18 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{31}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{31}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362175 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1069 "q-97725" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1072 #uuid "9db0917c-8f13-58c9-905e-e64b30cde0b1" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362247 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362151 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1069 "q-97726" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1072 #uuid "cc7a4edb-0118-580e-bca0-ed1f0771c326" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362248 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362136 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1069 "q-9785" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1071 "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1072 #uuid "a38c82cd-ff4b-53d8-85bc-f102ff90d451" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362249 1074 "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361991 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1069 "q-97871" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1071 "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- $B$ is invertible" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1072 #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362250 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362048 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1069 "q-97897" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1071 "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1072 #uuid "ed9c37d8-c5de-5898-be3f-6a097e562da9" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362251 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361989 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1069 "q-97915" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1071 "![](images/25/25d8d89ac4fdb3937596606d3ac347e08e3492c3dca65c3886c6eca8e3e121b5.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1072 #uuid "bc3b6302-4ca3-5f6e-a0d7-8bbb8a60978c" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362252 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1,y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=-1,0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/2b/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362167 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1069 "q-97918" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1071 "![](images/bf/bf8eb89b9fc5e0fa09e783f5774282decc778b6fe8ddc18e1a47c71a68505734.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1072 #uuid "127f057a-d203-5362-ab5e-58e0492959fa" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362253 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/d9/d970e9a23302139dfc40db62a8a2697f2a2d68b7523b40e0f8268b587eb904fb.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362186 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1069 "q-97945" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1072 #uuid "b1b3df2e-4878-5f07-9682-4c09eb922bca" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362254 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362147 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1069 "q-97946" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(B-4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(B-4I)=4$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1072 #uuid "6fdd6535-d1b6-5f9c-9675-a9cfd3d10476" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362255 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=B