efore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362178 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1069 "q-98665" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1072 #uuid "61a53a3d-9f1a-5b06-bcde-4575632a0617" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362313 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362200 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1069 "q-98668" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1072 #uuid "a52b4e8f-8a80-5361-a7aa-97f65400f662" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362314 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ),\\,{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\pi },\\,-{e}^{\\pi }⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times {e}^{\\pi }⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi })}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\,0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ \\,0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi })}^{3}}{\\,2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362082 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1069 "q-98682" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1071 "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1072 #uuid "d07e9501-4562-5f84-9934-b4d893bcda8e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362315 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:  \n\n![](images/84/848cf1a33161ac9fb25282cadc2f469675198a69b1df4a04d5eb88c15f66ebcc.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362176 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1069 "q-98690" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1071 "![](images/0d/0db1568fab302ddbd9f16dbdf9c276eeb62cbac37346b077c1639fe68c2396ac.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1072 #uuid "0317a51a-f6d5-582a-aa69-b3f89cc6775d" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362316 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362198 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1069 "q-98697" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1072 #uuid "657ec21d-728e-5f9c-8336-09507044d024" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362317 1074 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4x-2,\\,x(x-2),\\,x)=x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=2$ and $x=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{6x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-6 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-6 & =3x-6 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362142 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1069 "q-98699" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}=\\frac{1}{w-3}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1072 #uuid "305f4a20-a5d5-5769-aa7d-36e413111285" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362318 1074 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,\\,w(w-3),\\,w-3)=w(w-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ w(w-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}) & =w(w-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2w(w-3)}{w}+\\frac{3w(w-3)}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{w(w-3)}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{w}(w-3)}{\\cancel{w}}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{w(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w(w-3)}} & =\\frac{w\\cdot \\cancel{(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w-3}} \\\\ 2w-6+3 & =w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2w-6+3 & =w \\\\ w & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $w=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362010 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1069 "q-98700" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1071 "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a node" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1072 #uuid "dd0fe6f6-6555-578d-880a-04a4380b3213" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362319 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362203 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1069 "q-98701" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1071 "![](images/74/74790c4dbcb2d9cebee802dd155b5fa0a94e442f9bbc59c6277eb0d6fca3d60a.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=-5$ is a node\n- $y=-5$ is a sink" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1072 #uuid "b3fbe51b-8dce-56ec-a759-a07e5a4e34b0" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362320 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-5$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-5.$ This means that $y=-5$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362114 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1069 "q-98702" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1071 "![](images/68/68eb28d31145fe38d481cb2273bc35edc80bfccf1b80b2e9a75b4136eab45629.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source\n- $y=4$ is a node\n- $y=1$ is a sink" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1072 #uuid "bf8ec5e0-4855-5481-a220-299eb2c89a02" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362321 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=4$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=4.$ This means that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side oft $y=1.$This means that $y=1$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362058 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1069 "q-98706" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=1$ and  $y=3$ are equilibrium points\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the phase line of the differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1072 #uuid "a4874a14-fde7-58db-a9f6-f09b704708e8" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362322 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each condition:\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ and $y=3$ are equilibrium points. We represent this on the phase line by drawing two distinct points at these locations.\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\nTherefore, our phase line is as follows:\n\n![](images/80/804857317470b8ba85edb165770e80511eaab144f76681c615625f8d5c6e8b5e.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362202 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1069 "q-98708" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1072 #uuid "5692f221-3e0b-5059-a8e0-07dee6b9fc2a" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362323 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362092 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1069 "q-98714" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1071 "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1072 #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362324 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362003 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1069 "q-98715" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1071 "![](images/4a/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1072 #uuid "d67a28ee-e52e-5e0c-b902-450c792552a6" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362325 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362174 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1069 "q-98716" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1072 #uuid "c2197596-2744-5571-ba37-b2f03d9b6d9e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362326 1074 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,\\,x+2,\\,x(x+2))=x(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ x(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}) & =x(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2x(x+2)}{x}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{4x(x+2)}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x}}-\\frac{x\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{x(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x(x+2)}} \\\\ 2(x+2)-x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)-x & =4 \\\\ 2x+4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362046 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1069 "q-98718" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{{N}^{2}+N}=\\frac{1}{N}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1072 #uuid "4905c9a2-8f0f-5b29-bf59-233598ac900a" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362327 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)}=\\frac{1}{N}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(N,\\,N+1,\\,N(N+1))=N(N+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $N=0$ and $N=-1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)} & =\\frac{1}{N} \\\\ N(N+1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{N(N+1)}) & =N(N+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{N} \\\\ \\frac{2N(N+1)}{N+1}-\\frac{N(N+1)}{N(N+1)} & =\\frac{N(N+1)}{N} \\\\ \\frac{2N\\cdot \\cancel{(N+1)}}{\\cancel{N+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{N(N+1)}}{\\cancel{N(N+1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{N}\\cdot (N+1)}{\\cancel{N}} \\\\ 2N-1 & =N+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $N,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2N-1 & =N+1 \\\\ N & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne 0$ and $2\\ne -1,$ we conclude that $N=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $N=2.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362050 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1069 "q-98752" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1071 "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1072 #uuid "99cb8df0-a236-544f-967a-a702cdee4d6f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362328 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362205 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1069 "q-98757" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1072 #uuid "bd711689-2039-5e3a-97e9-854277e0ac5b" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362329 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/df/df68345e3424f1e0020a98ef8dd13456865412818067a822c01c3f50e49386ec.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362045 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1069 "q-98758" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-6?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1072 #uuid "302fd178-9e1c-5e26-bc1f-3fc86fe5e86e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362330 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-6 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as f