um solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just below $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just above it.\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just below $y=3$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just above it.\n\n![](images/3d/3d2eec912d188cd569c4f87558a148c808ee2c815eeca7289103f11465d98859.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d11dbd5ae88f62511de12bf4745fc055d59f5ef20de6125685217d87e5e08b17.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to diverge away from this line." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362035 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1069 "q-98790" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1072 #uuid "b55f37e8-6033-5c54-8e71-4349b9fb7b3e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362338 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](images/d0/d03ab1d15be3656787d7ccd0114b2011f561bfed9c67d22f3366f50ed2f410e4.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/89/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361996 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1069 "q-98946" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,i+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1072 #uuid "4992c928-66f9-55e4-9096-4efa99debc55" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362339 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362063 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1069 "q-9896" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1071 "What is the radius of convergence $R$ of the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{x}^{n}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\,?\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1072 #uuid "0b0b5805-3e8b-5e97-815d-db00ae3f1a01" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362340 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={x}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|x| \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362042 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1069 "q-99006" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1071 "Solve the differential equation $-5{f}^{′}(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=0.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1072 #uuid "d1695f8e-4929-5966-a2ac-c737d784a8b6" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362341 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362180 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1069 "q-99019" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1071 "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1072 #uuid "cf3198cc-6d1f-53ed-9066-c8e5e92b5438" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362342 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (3x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362131 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1069 "q-99027" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-x-y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1072 #uuid "8339a664-cc92-5ae4-a4f3-d6ab19787d80" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362343 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362037 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1069 "q-99030" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1072 #uuid "47aa66c7-074f-539e-9248-f6b2a1659ce1" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362344 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362115 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1069 "q-99039" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x+16y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1072 #uuid "10fa34d1-bf77-58e6-9792-74938feb848e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362345 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362135 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1069 "q-9911" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1071 "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1072 #uuid "e554c090-e3bc-5a93-90b4-9f79a0997b62" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362346 1074 "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}k & -k \\\\ k & k\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}|+k|\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({k}^{2}+{k}^{2})+(k+5k)+k(k-5k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k+k\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-4{k}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{k}^{2}+6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{k}^{2}+6k & =0 \\\\ k(-2k+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=3.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362133 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1069 "q-99112" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={5}^{x+3}+4?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1072 #uuid "3d5053b5-b8c9-548f-9879-3ddacdfaa62c" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362347 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e4/e422248a8c0b44250355c897b7825baedcdd7285a13f4547180e4f21e14e7d90.png)\n\n- To plot $y={5}^{x+3},$ we shift the graph of $y={5}^{x}$ horizontally by $3$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-3,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed40db9640d07155f5d75f37e20f52005ef7de97028089c5335ae5445b72499.png)\n\n- To plot $y={5}^{x+3}+4,$ we shift the graph of $y={5}^{x+3}$ vertically by $4$ units up. The point $(-3,1)$ moves to $(-3,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/29/29e8fe8c17324bff60e076f268f771896ac990a2c559348b985aef2af48a7467.png)" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362194 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1069 "q-99163" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1071 "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1072 #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362348 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362145 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1069 "q-99168" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1072 #uuid "50c63a8a-ed83-5245-b469-aa943d591180" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362349 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362080 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1069 "q-99171" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+6y+10z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1072 #uuid "2d3ecce9-3af4-5e04-a08c-80960443b54f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362350 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362025 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1069 "q-9920" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1072 #uuid "d1bfb84c-9164-5c45-a8da-378584db30de" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362351 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362091 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1069 "q-99240" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1072 #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362352 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362165 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1069 "q-99253" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨4{t}^{3}-1,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1072 #uuid "f0cd1cc2-ffd2-5c86-9cfc-13629a175dfe" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362353 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨4{t}^{3}-1,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨4{t}^{3}-1,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (4{t}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,2\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}-t+{c}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}-t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}-t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362079 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1069 "q-99255" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1072 #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362354 1074 "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x & =2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y & =2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362173 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1069 "q-99260" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1072 #uuid "397033be-95da-5ec4-b034-439a24a48658" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362355 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362102 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1069 "q-99263" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1072 #uuid "df203c10-a500-551f-a8c3-868e39ddc288" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362356 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362162 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1069 "q-99264" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1072 #uuid "0b1c2371-6e4d-55bf-8462-352cb3ad4deb" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362357 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\text{ }5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362041 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1069 "q-99265" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,{e}^{t},1⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,1,-{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1072 #uuid "004047e8-481a-52bf-93e0-48e2a037481f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362358 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,1,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-{e}^{2t}-1,-(0-0),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-({e}^{2t}+1),0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361999 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1069 "q-99268" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1071 "$\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1072 #uuid "9b3d9582-d12a-5267-be26-557c95efa4d5" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362359 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362078 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1069 "q-99274" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1072 #uuid "7eb8a72b-0beb-5d49-b077-ac32cdeca74e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362360 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362116 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1069 "q-99275" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12}).$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1072 #uuid "b0160257-6d01-50d3-a643-02b7249aff4d" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362361 1074 "The graph $y={x}^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{12}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $8$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362208 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1069 "q-99286" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1072 #uuid "918aac5f-f716-5fb3-ac75-edc776a3f29a" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362362 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{1}-2{e}^{0},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2e-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361985 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1069 "q-99300" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{({t}^{2}+1)}^{4}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1072 #uuid "f4e88c3a-1837-567e-aaf3-80262168b8a0" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362363 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2}+1)^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2})^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{8} \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362026 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1069 "q-99313" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1072 #uuid "5c0e0acc-fc9f-5b16-9c89-6c5bbccf58b2" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362364 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -(-1) & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-1.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2+(-1)\\text{i}=-2-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3+0\\text{i} \\\\ 5+(-2)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2∓\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5∓2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361986 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1069 "q-9932" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1071 "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\frac{1}{1+x},$\n given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯\\,,\\,x\\in (-1,1)\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1072 #uuid "ec4ce95b-9e52-5c36-9436-78a68133d431" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362365 1074 "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-x \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =(-1)^{0}{x}^{0} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =(-1)^{1}{x}^{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =(-1)^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =(-1)^{3}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1+x}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362020 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1069 "q-99329" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1072 #uuid "36d2e97b-bfbd-5b5a-8af5-ecab59e35588" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362366 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0+(-2)\\text{i}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4+1\\text{i} \\\\ -3+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ -3∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362023 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1069 "q-99342" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1072 #uuid "33cf532b-66bb-5817-8de3-f7bd5092526d" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362367 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362101 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1069 "q-99352" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1071 "Find $\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}⟩\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1072 #uuid "b2e00c3f-3c92-5abd-a940-e387ea360e1f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362368 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362155 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1069 "q-99378" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1071 "Find $\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1072 #uuid "a8b47072-f658-5399-9e57-e737d1e1fce4" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362369 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=2{e}^{(2t+1)},$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{u}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362021 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362370 1069 "q-99411" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362370 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and 