_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362024 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1069 "q-9942" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1071 "If $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1072 #uuid "a85bd741-601d-56b9-acc2-25f554507b6a" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362373 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2))+({x}^{2})(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362017 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362374 1069 "q-99420" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362374 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩, \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362374 10