 point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={sin}^{3}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)({sin}^{3}⁡x)+({x}^{2})(3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{sin}^{3}⁡x+3{x}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=0} & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡(0)+3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362181 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1069 "q-99469" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1072 #uuid "bb6d22f5-37c8-5d1a-9e4f-59c3f6e96912" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362377 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(2-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362138 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1069 "q-99478" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{6}^{x}-4}{{3}^{x}+c}=4,$ what is the value of constant $c?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1072 #uuid "3daf626b-8367-56db-bbf2-291cffb4791d" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362378 1074 "We can evaluate this limit in terms of the constant $c$ using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{6}^{x}-4}{{3}^{x}+c} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({6}^{x}-4)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({3}^{x}+c)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-4}{{3}^{2}+c} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{9+c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $4,$ we can equate the above with $4,$ and then solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{9+c} & =4 \\\\ 9+c & =8 \\\\ c & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362030 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1069 "q-99480" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1072 #uuid "7f1be8b8-3745-5a36-93d3-1a606462f05e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362379 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1-2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -13 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -13 & -3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -13{x}_{1}+(-3+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-3-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361994 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1069 "q-99505" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x}=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1072 #uuid "def34be0-5343-5a7a-b63c-7b336175d666" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362380 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f9cf17608e32b91f4efbb243702286b484938c015f74f0b59ca906dc24c593a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362209 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1069 "q-99507" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x})=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1072 #uuid "b5d93a21-4b46-5cff-b9c3-bf3f9cd7e0d9" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362381 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x}) & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({2}^{-1})^{x} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d3477662fd9a07c6f1543728a1562f4fed62f91350741078a7d568f500d32d0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}=-9\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362068 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1069 "q-99515" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7{e}^{3x-8}}{3}=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1072 #uuid "307360ec-3549-51f5-9f1c-3956163e2641" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362382 1074 "We can use the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7{e}^{3x-8}}{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={e}^{3x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df39137953015b819f3143e823f042ac759a8dfb356821ce6f623b40924b0b0c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362125 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1069 "q-99517" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3(t-1)^{2},4\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩$ and $F(1)=⟨1,\\,1⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1072 #uuid "d68c695f-2c41-537c-9ad8-b7156433decc" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362383 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3(t-1)^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-1⟩\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3(t-1)^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-1⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 3(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4{t}^{3}-1)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{(t-1)}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{t}^{4}-t+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨1,\\,1⟩$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨{((1)-1)}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,(1)^{4}-(1)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨{(t-1)}^{3}+1,\\,{t}^{4}-t+1⟩.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362074 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1069 "q-99586" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩$ and $F(1)=⟨2,\\,0⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1072 #uuid "66eee9f4-0ecf-5776-b383-87a4b244cb4f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362384 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨2,\\,0⟩$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{2}^{1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{7}{6}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{5}{6}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$ Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}+3{t}^{2}+5}{6},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}-2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362095 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1069 "q-9961" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1071 "Given that the determinant of  $[\\begin{aligned}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is $-6$, find the value of $n.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1072 #uuid "ec95474f-3d89-5428-8221-da33b63b3f3b" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362385 1074 "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4-n\\cdot 2+0 \\\\  & =4-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-6,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2n & =-6 \\\\ -2n & =-10 \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362146 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1069 "q-99635" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1072 #uuid "029fdb6a-d6ee-5a1f-bdd5-c75825b67c96" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362386 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-1)(1)+2(1)(2)+(1)(-3) \\\\  & =(-1)+4-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(1)+2(-1)(2)+(2)(-3) \\\\  & =2-4-6 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362164 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1069 "q-99653" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1071 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1072 #uuid "2a4d1e60-e5ad-568f-b928-a3604e33809e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362387 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362055 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1069 "q-99656" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1072 #uuid "689e4d4d-2277-5c62-9cd6-9887b81429d8" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362388 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362018 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1069 "q-9967" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1072 #uuid "7e033a7c-c230-5700-a5b4-d8c955024d86" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362389 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =4|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362201 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1069 "q-99724" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1071 "![](images/cd/cdce64bf5d02850bbedc2b89af3b2e7447b733831c09d1955e4a9ee97e82d556.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1072 #uuid "10464afa-e24d-5bb8-8ed8-ff334320cdee" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362390 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Hence, $c=2.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2 & =4 \\\\ k+2 & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362052 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1069 "q-99774" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1071 "![](images/d8/d80c45f952e4c8a65349be524849104b1a76e6ee276186646c6c3facd83c8181.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={7}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1072 #uuid "b018fdf1-c3ab-56d5-97f8-9a97dabdfd94" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362391 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={7}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={7}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362150 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1069 "q-99775" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1071 "![](images/92/929706c015fd38e83c9529a28203584a4e3e53a204ac3b4d20539d090a3fd25a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={12}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1072 #uuid "d8e58eb8-2e35-57c5-8905-f16a49b3cc0e" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362392 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={12}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362120 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1069 "q-99778" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1071 "![](images/51/5119e42566eff8078bdcf38d8c521e291f902bf1f03f6ddb00321989e3c94c5a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({5}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1072 #uuid "4aab5f63-2056-5d00-a333-f4b526199883" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362393 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({5}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({5}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({5}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({5}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({5}^{x})-2.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362087 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1069 "q-9978" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1071 "![](images/79/79889a1366311f6175a3cc736d5cd800a19a9d843daffc824d36c0690f0e3310.png)\n\nFind the area of the closed region between the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above." 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1072 #uuid "da377ea7-a436-56fd-bc10-dd4b8618d147" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362394 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(2-(-2))-(\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{8}{3})) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362086 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1069 "q-9980" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1071 "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1072 #uuid "3e095599-c87c-5424-992d-faffcd2cd697" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362395 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(k-2)-3(4-6)+1(4-3k) \\\\  & =2k-4+6+4-3k \\\\  & =6-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-k}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{6-k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =2(6-k) \\\\ 10 & =12-2k \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362117 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1069 "q-99815" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1072 #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362396 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362051 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1069 "q-99822" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{2}⟩=0.$*" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1072 #uuid "5c3132dd-8104-5475-a04a-6ba3130fe419" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362397 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362159 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1069 "q-99827" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1072 #uuid "30fad2b1-33b1-5011-baca-2c53469ec04d" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362398 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533434 17