{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362006 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1069 "q-99875" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1072 #uuid "021a928d-5bbc-5ce6-b2a2-f571107e0c32" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362401 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(1)+3(-1)(3)+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =1-9+8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(3)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(-1)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362005 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1069 "q-99878" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1072 #uuid "8a41208f-2a14-503d-a1ad-b859e476f0c0" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362402 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(5)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =10-2-8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+(1)(2)+(2)(-1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(5)(0)+(-2)(2)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0-4+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362044 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1069 "q-99951" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1072 #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362403 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362109 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1069 "q-99959" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1071 "Find the limit at $x=-1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ -2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=-1 \\\\ x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1072 #uuid "7138ff6a-d953-5754-874b-151b53514bb5" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362404 1074 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361990 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1069 "q-99961" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1072 #uuid "91708f0d-366e-5441-a87d-7b80b150a761" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362405 1074 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+c) \\\\  & =-1+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 1 & =-1+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361997 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1069 "q-99967" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ x & \\text{ for }\\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1072 #uuid "51903a6c-9b81-599d-828f-e154abbaa305" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362406 1074 "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the left-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362108 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1069 "q-99968" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1072 #uuid "ba0326a7-b201-50b8-850b-0b5749c27ef3" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362407 1074 "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186361998 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1069 "q-9998" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1071 "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1072 #uuid "90452d65-c7b3-548e-83d9-2c700d6e9c0f" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true] [17592186362408 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2(2k-0)+2(k-0) \\\\  & =-4k+2k \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533434 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0034a4c6-10b3-508b-8a76-afcee44b5628" 17592186360835 "answer-00559944-b824-562d-ab1f-1d6d34ee529e" 17592186360836 "answer-00a864a5-90fe-5a19-9ac4-88e0769c548c" 17592186360837 "answer-00bbaa91-9d31-5875-9261-e253520000fd" 17592186360838 "answer-00bd5f87-14a5-5338-93e2-44c6cf9b16e4" 17592186360839 "answer-00c248fd-f901-5734-8111-61613aba8041" 17592186360840 "answer-012be593-438d-5ae0-aea5-c3417daadd12" 17592186360841 "answer-014f5699-9400-5499-a42f-6b0b1436ca5d" 1759218